00:26:59.740 --> 00:27:01.540
So firstly, welcome.

00:27:01.540 --> 00:27:06.340
I think right now we live
in, quite possibly, one

00:27:06.340 --> 00:27:08.740
of the most exciting
times that I can remember

00:27:08.740 --> 00:27:13.390
in terms of the
evolution of technology

00:27:13.390 --> 00:27:18.560
and how it is intersecting
with business and with life.

00:27:18.560 --> 00:27:21.920
I think we saw with
the example of Metro,

00:27:21.920 --> 00:27:25.950
there is a real
transformation taking place.

00:27:25.950 --> 00:27:29.270
This is not just about
technology allowing

00:27:29.270 --> 00:27:33.290
you to do things faster,
cheaper, and more

00:27:33.290 --> 00:27:36.660
reliably than you could before.

00:27:36.660 --> 00:27:41.040
This is not about
incremental change.

00:27:41.040 --> 00:27:43.560
Absolutely, you
can use technology

00:27:43.560 --> 00:27:48.630
to make it cheaper,
faster, and more reliable,

00:27:48.630 --> 00:27:53.540
but technology today is being
used to do things differently,

00:27:53.540 --> 00:27:56.280
fundamentally differently.

00:27:56.280 --> 00:28:00.020
And this is what digital
transformation is all about.

00:28:00.020 --> 00:28:03.350
This is not about
business as usual.

00:28:03.350 --> 00:28:06.380
This is not about
government as usual.

00:28:06.380 --> 00:28:08.970
This is about doing
things differently.

00:28:08.970 --> 00:28:13.650
It's about thinking
about things differently.

00:28:13.650 --> 00:28:18.810
Every company in this world
becomes a technology business.

00:28:18.810 --> 00:28:22.160
It's about writing software.

00:28:22.160 --> 00:28:25.000
It's about getting intimacy
and insight into your customers

00:28:25.000 --> 00:28:27.420
and the world around you.

00:28:27.420 --> 00:28:31.430
This is what the digital
transformation is all about.

00:28:31.430 --> 00:28:35.540
And it's driven by a set
of technology trends.

00:28:35.540 --> 00:28:38.400
Many of them have been
evolving over many years,

00:28:38.400 --> 00:28:43.380
but they've all come together
at this point in time

00:28:43.380 --> 00:28:46.900
to enable true
transformation and change.

00:28:46.900 --> 00:28:48.849
If you think about Cloud--

00:28:48.849 --> 00:28:51.890
for me, Cloud is
not a destination.

00:28:51.890 --> 00:28:56.150
Cloud is a vehicle that takes us
on this digital transformation

00:28:56.150 --> 00:28:57.350
journey.

00:28:57.350 --> 00:29:00.170
Cloud-- with the
first time with Cloud,

00:29:00.170 --> 00:29:03.890
you can get technology, and
particularly the technology

00:29:03.890 --> 00:29:08.110
that doesn't differentiate
you, as a commodity.

00:29:08.110 --> 00:29:11.080
In the past you had
to buy software.

00:29:11.080 --> 00:29:13.210
You had to buy the
hardware to run it on.

00:29:13.210 --> 00:29:16.330
You had to buy the people
to run the software

00:29:16.330 --> 00:29:18.080
and run the hardware.

00:29:18.080 --> 00:29:20.980
Now you can make it
someone else's problem

00:29:20.980 --> 00:29:25.450
so that you can focus on
what differentiates you, what

00:29:25.450 --> 00:29:28.940
makes you more competitive.

00:29:28.940 --> 00:29:32.960
This is what Cloud allows.

00:29:32.960 --> 00:29:38.430
With mobility, you can now
engage with anyone anywhere.

00:29:38.430 --> 00:29:41.070
You know, you can make good
guesses as to what they're

00:29:41.070 --> 00:29:43.320
doing, what they're
doing in their lives,

00:29:43.320 --> 00:29:44.730
you're getting intimacy.

00:29:44.730 --> 00:29:48.670
And from that intimacy, you can
get insight and understanding

00:29:48.670 --> 00:29:51.640
so that you can deliver
better services,

00:29:51.640 --> 00:29:54.480
whether that's monetizing ads
or whether it's allowing people

00:29:54.480 --> 00:29:56.190
to do amazing things.

00:29:56.190 --> 00:29:59.280
You see governments who put
applications on phones so

00:29:59.280 --> 00:30:02.400
that their customers,
their citizens,

00:30:02.400 --> 00:30:06.090
can take photographs of potholes
in the road in Malaysia,

00:30:06.090 --> 00:30:08.850
and they automatically
end up on a list

00:30:08.850 --> 00:30:11.910
of things to be done by
the Ministry of Transport.

00:30:11.910 --> 00:30:14.490
Mobility is changing everything.

00:30:14.490 --> 00:30:16.500
Social is changing everything.

00:30:16.500 --> 00:30:20.340
The interaction model, the way
people engage with each other,

00:30:20.340 --> 00:30:22.110
is shifting and changing.

00:30:22.110 --> 00:30:24.450
In some ways we talk a
lot about globalization,

00:30:24.450 --> 00:30:27.240
and we think about it
in terms of industry,

00:30:27.240 --> 00:30:30.720
but there's a globalization
of friendships.

00:30:30.720 --> 00:30:37.490
My daughter, well, one of
my four daughters, is 25.

00:30:37.490 --> 00:30:38.440
She's from England.

00:30:38.440 --> 00:30:40.010
She met friends online.

00:30:40.010 --> 00:30:44.570
She went partying with many
of them, actually in Poland,

00:30:44.570 --> 00:30:46.340
and now she's moved
to Australia, where

00:30:46.340 --> 00:30:48.902
a whole bunch of them live.

00:30:48.902 --> 00:30:51.360
But this whole-- the way people
talk to each other, the way

00:30:51.360 --> 00:30:55.170
people engage, the way people
share information and trends

00:30:55.170 --> 00:30:58.150
are driven, is fundamentally
shifting and changing.

00:30:58.150 --> 00:31:00.210
IoT gives that same
connection that you

00:31:00.210 --> 00:31:03.330
see with social and mobile,
but not with people,

00:31:03.330 --> 00:31:05.550
but with machines.

00:31:05.550 --> 00:31:07.230
I'm connected.

00:31:07.230 --> 00:31:08.220
I'm a cyborg.

00:31:08.220 --> 00:31:10.200
Did you know that?

00:31:10.200 --> 00:31:13.680
I happen to be diabetic, and I
have a constant flow glucometer

00:31:13.680 --> 00:31:15.780
plumbed into my arm.

00:31:15.780 --> 00:31:19.920
That allows my wife, 5,000
miles away in the United States,

00:31:19.920 --> 00:31:21.870
to know that I am healthy.

00:31:21.870 --> 00:31:25.860
And I can monitor my 8-year-old
daughter, who is also diabetic.

00:31:25.860 --> 00:31:29.070
And if my wife is asleep
and my daughter goes low,

00:31:29.070 --> 00:31:30.660
I can give her a
call and wake her up

00:31:30.660 --> 00:31:33.172
so that there
isn't an emergency.

00:31:33.172 --> 00:31:35.780
This connectedness
is allowing us

00:31:35.780 --> 00:31:39.760
to get more data and insight.

00:31:39.760 --> 00:31:42.190
And all of that, big data.

00:31:42.190 --> 00:31:45.880
The job of technologists is
to turn that data into insight

00:31:45.880 --> 00:31:49.090
and understanding,
and then act on it,

00:31:49.090 --> 00:31:51.460
to be able to
engage, again, back

00:31:51.460 --> 00:31:54.820
through the internet of
things or with mobile.

00:31:54.820 --> 00:31:58.180
This intersection of all
of these technologies

00:31:58.180 --> 00:32:01.060
is profoundly changing
everything we do in life,

00:32:01.060 --> 00:32:04.300
and it says that every
organization and every business

00:32:04.300 --> 00:32:07.480
today is a technology business.

00:32:07.480 --> 00:32:12.820
Your success will be defined
by how you use and consume

00:32:12.820 --> 00:32:17.320
technology, and your
failure, or your epitaph

00:32:17.320 --> 00:32:19.140
on your gravestone,
will be how you

00:32:19.140 --> 00:32:23.350
didn't, if you don't
take advantage of it.

00:32:23.350 --> 00:32:25.720
What we see happening
with this technology

00:32:25.720 --> 00:32:28.460
is technology is being used.

00:32:28.460 --> 00:32:31.480
It is being used to
undermine the assumptions,

00:32:31.480 --> 00:32:36.490
the foundations, that you
build business models on.

00:32:36.490 --> 00:32:38.600
I'll give you a
couple of examples.

00:32:38.600 --> 00:32:40.330
The first one is a
fairly famous one.

00:32:40.330 --> 00:32:42.610
Most of you will be
familiar with the stories

00:32:42.610 --> 00:32:46.730
of ride sharing and
things like Uber and Lyft.

00:32:46.730 --> 00:32:51.350
What's interesting here is the
story of how it came about.

00:32:51.350 --> 00:32:56.330
So taxis, you know, it's an
old established business.

00:32:56.330 --> 00:32:59.650
And there's certain barriers
to entry to the market place.

00:32:59.650 --> 00:33:02.060
If you want to be
a taxi driver, you

00:33:02.060 --> 00:33:03.950
have to learn your
way around cities

00:33:03.950 --> 00:33:09.220
because driving with a map in
your lap is kind of dangerous.

00:33:09.220 --> 00:33:10.780
And you kind of want
to know that you

00:33:10.780 --> 00:33:13.720
can trust the taxi driver.

00:33:13.720 --> 00:33:16.330
So in London, they have a
thing called the Knowledge,

00:33:16.330 --> 00:33:19.000
and you spend three
years studying

00:33:19.000 --> 00:33:23.279
all the streets and the
landmarks around London.

00:33:23.279 --> 00:33:24.820
And it's quite
interesting, actually.

00:33:24.820 --> 00:33:29.050
So these taxi drivers-- all
this memorization and recall

00:33:29.050 --> 00:33:30.520
actually turns
them into mutants.

00:33:33.630 --> 00:33:36.930
So it turns out that when
you memorize things so much,

00:33:36.930 --> 00:33:39.060
it actually makes a
part of your brain

00:33:39.060 --> 00:33:43.360
bigger, physically bigger.

00:33:43.360 --> 00:33:45.030
So the Darwin in
me says that if we

00:33:45.030 --> 00:33:47.250
look at taxi drivers
for long enough,

00:33:47.250 --> 00:33:50.040
they'll end up with
these enormous heads,

00:33:50.040 --> 00:33:53.520
just like those aliens that you
see in 1950s science fiction

00:33:53.520 --> 00:33:56.240
movies.

00:33:56.240 --> 00:33:59.180
The point is that these
businesses in the underlying

00:33:59.180 --> 00:34:03.320
assumptions were predicated on
teaching drivers and validating

00:34:03.320 --> 00:34:05.040
them through licensing.

00:34:05.040 --> 00:34:09.400
Now, with a mobile device,
it has GPS, it has voice,

00:34:09.400 --> 00:34:12.750
it has maps, it can
tell you where to go.

00:34:12.750 --> 00:34:15.420
You can summon your ride,
you can see where it is,

00:34:15.420 --> 00:34:18.389
you can give feedback at the
end so that you get a better

00:34:18.389 --> 00:34:22.139
idea of the reputation of
the driver than you ever,

00:34:22.139 --> 00:34:25.130
ever could before.

00:34:25.130 --> 00:34:27.199
This is a fundamental shift.

00:34:27.199 --> 00:34:31.489
This is technology disrupting
established businesses.

00:34:31.489 --> 00:34:34.520
If you think of the spare parts
business and the logistics,

00:34:34.520 --> 00:34:38.420
3D printing means you don't
need warehouses full of spares

00:34:38.420 --> 00:34:39.889
anymore.

00:34:39.889 --> 00:34:43.040
You can print them on demand
with 3D printers and CNC

00:34:43.040 --> 00:34:45.340
machines at the edge.

00:34:45.340 --> 00:34:48.790
This disruption is
coming to all of us

00:34:48.790 --> 00:34:51.730
where technology is
being used and it says,

00:34:51.730 --> 00:34:55.210
is that assumption that your
model is built on valid anymore

00:34:55.210 --> 00:34:57.040
in the face of technology?

00:34:57.040 --> 00:35:00.430
And when it isn't,
then there's risk,

00:35:00.430 --> 00:35:03.430
because the small and
nimble, with nothing to lose,

00:35:03.430 --> 00:35:06.880
have access to the same
technologies as the largest

00:35:06.880 --> 00:35:08.560
businesses in the world.

00:35:08.560 --> 00:35:11.990
And that is because of Cloud.

00:35:11.990 --> 00:35:14.210
So when we think about
the journey going forward,

00:35:14.210 --> 00:35:15.876
the question is, what
does it look like?

00:35:15.876 --> 00:35:17.780
What are the steps?

00:35:17.780 --> 00:35:19.820
We put it down into
four key areas.

00:35:19.820 --> 00:35:21.590
The first, optimize.

00:35:21.590 --> 00:35:23.180
Get out of the
business of delivering

00:35:23.180 --> 00:35:25.520
IT that doesn't
differentiate you,

00:35:25.520 --> 00:35:27.350
and make it someone
else's problem.

00:35:27.350 --> 00:35:29.690
Make it Google's
problem, because what

00:35:29.690 --> 00:35:33.290
doesn't differentiate you
does differentiate us.

00:35:33.290 --> 00:35:36.140
So that you can focus
on collaboration,

00:35:36.140 --> 00:35:39.440
driving teams to work together
to be more productive and more

00:35:39.440 --> 00:35:42.140
effective, and through
that collaboration,

00:35:42.140 --> 00:35:45.060
drive innovation.

00:35:45.060 --> 00:35:46.800
And then to accelerate
your business,

00:35:46.800 --> 00:35:49.290
to be able to focus
on the technology that

00:35:49.290 --> 00:35:52.770
enables insight and
engagement to actually drive

00:35:52.770 --> 00:35:57.610
new value, new services,
and to take them to market.

00:35:57.610 --> 00:36:00.570
And then in doing all of that,
you need the right partner.

00:36:00.570 --> 00:36:03.420
We believe we are
that partner to help

00:36:03.420 --> 00:36:07.000
you take that digital
transformation journey.

00:36:07.000 --> 00:36:09.080
Let's quickly talk
about optimize

00:36:09.080 --> 00:36:11.040
and what's happening
in that space.

00:36:11.040 --> 00:36:13.080
For us, Google Cloud
is a full suite

00:36:13.080 --> 00:36:16.320
of products going from
the low level of offering

00:36:16.320 --> 00:36:19.200
compute network and
storage all the way up

00:36:19.200 --> 00:36:23.640
to work collaboration
suites like G Suite.

00:36:23.640 --> 00:36:25.980
When we talk to our customers,
there's a set of drivers

00:36:25.980 --> 00:36:26.550
that we see.

00:36:26.550 --> 00:36:29.060
Reliability, security,
and so forth.

00:36:29.060 --> 00:36:34.620
It's the classic list that IT
want out of infrastructure.

00:36:34.620 --> 00:36:37.372
Google, what doesn't
differentiate you,

00:36:37.372 --> 00:36:39.330
you want to get us
infrastructure as a service.

00:36:39.330 --> 00:36:43.020
I say is infrastructure is
a differentiator for us.

00:36:43.020 --> 00:36:46.560
We believe, and our businesses,
all of those billion user

00:36:46.560 --> 00:36:50.100
businesses, have driven us to
deliver better infrastructure,

00:36:50.100 --> 00:36:50.820
more scalable.

00:36:50.820 --> 00:36:53.620
In fact, we build
our own servers.

00:36:53.620 --> 00:36:55.140
If we sold servers,
we'd probably

00:36:55.140 --> 00:36:58.200
be the world's third or
fourth largest server vendor,

00:36:58.200 --> 00:37:00.180
but we don't sell any.

00:37:00.180 --> 00:37:02.640
They're all built and
customized for Google

00:37:02.640 --> 00:37:05.610
so that we can deliver services
that are more reliable,

00:37:05.610 --> 00:37:09.900
that are faster, and cheaper
than we could in any other way.

00:37:09.900 --> 00:37:13.150
And we were driven to do that by
the growth of our own business.

00:37:13.150 --> 00:37:16.920
And the opportunity for you
is to take advantage of that.

00:37:16.920 --> 00:37:19.950
It's also about managing
things in a different way.

00:37:19.950 --> 00:37:21.375
With site reliability,
engineering

00:37:21.375 --> 00:37:24.270
is about taking us to a
place when we build out

00:37:24.270 --> 00:37:26.490
infrastructure in
this new world,

00:37:26.490 --> 00:37:28.990
we think differently
about availability.

00:37:28.990 --> 00:37:31.860
It's all about resilience
and reliability,

00:37:31.860 --> 00:37:36.740
and it's baking it into
everything that we do.

00:37:36.740 --> 00:37:38.480
The apps in this
new world assume

00:37:38.480 --> 00:37:40.160
the infrastructure breaks.

00:37:40.160 --> 00:37:42.560
So you have to build and
design them differently,

00:37:42.560 --> 00:37:44.450
and you have to manage
them differently,

00:37:44.450 --> 00:37:46.040
and we wrote the
book on it, something

00:37:46.040 --> 00:37:47.660
we shared with the world.

00:37:47.660 --> 00:37:49.640
And I encourage all
of you to actually

00:37:49.640 --> 00:37:51.980
get the site reliability
engineering book

00:37:51.980 --> 00:37:55.880
and to have a read and learn
how Google has figured out

00:37:55.880 --> 00:37:58.670
at least how to deliver
at extreme scale

00:37:58.670 --> 00:38:00.251
and with great reliability.

00:38:00.251 --> 00:38:02.750
I always say one of the amazing
things about Google that you

00:38:02.750 --> 00:38:05.510
can learn is we can afford
to make the mistakes that you

00:38:05.510 --> 00:38:06.770
can't.

00:38:06.770 --> 00:38:07.810
We've been very lucky.

00:38:07.810 --> 00:38:09.536
We have a very
successful business.

00:38:09.536 --> 00:38:10.910
And we've been
able to experiment

00:38:10.910 --> 00:38:12.800
and learn the lessons
that otherwise

00:38:12.800 --> 00:38:15.600
would be very expensive.

00:38:15.600 --> 00:38:17.700
In terms of delivering
performance,

00:38:17.700 --> 00:38:20.190
we have something rather unique.

00:38:20.190 --> 00:38:24.510
Google actually has a navy.

00:38:24.510 --> 00:38:27.070
I bet you didn't
know that, right?

00:38:27.070 --> 00:38:28.690
We actually have ships.

00:38:28.690 --> 00:38:33.420
And what those ships do is they
lay cables under the ocean.

00:38:33.420 --> 00:38:36.090
One of our main
differentiators is actually

00:38:36.090 --> 00:38:39.960
by having our own physical
network with the fibers that we

00:38:39.960 --> 00:38:42.780
own ourselves going
across the globe,

00:38:42.780 --> 00:38:45.690
we are in a position to make
your applications perform

00:38:45.690 --> 00:38:49.770
and be more reliable than
in any other infrastructure.

00:38:49.770 --> 00:38:52.680
You don't have to
worry about talking

00:38:52.680 --> 00:38:55.530
with Telco providers
for WAN connections

00:38:55.530 --> 00:38:57.480
because we can
give you very, very

00:38:57.480 --> 00:39:00.940
fat, fast, low latency
pipes across the world,

00:39:00.940 --> 00:39:03.060
so that when you build
applications and run them

00:39:03.060 --> 00:39:05.970
on Google Cloud, they
can talk to each other

00:39:05.970 --> 00:39:08.130
and get very, very low latency.

00:39:08.130 --> 00:39:12.030
And in fact, today, something
like 40% of the total world's

00:39:12.030 --> 00:39:18.254
internet traffic goes across
Google's own private network.

00:39:18.254 --> 00:39:19.170
This is pretty insane.

00:39:19.170 --> 00:39:20.545
By the way, when
I joined Google,

00:39:20.545 --> 00:39:22.800
I was like, insane engineering.

00:39:22.800 --> 00:39:23.550
Crazy numbers.

00:39:23.550 --> 00:39:25.920
We build a lot of our
own networking kit.

00:39:25.920 --> 00:39:28.480
To allow our customers to
take advantage of this,

00:39:28.480 --> 00:39:31.490
we also now have direct
connection capabilities

00:39:31.490 --> 00:39:34.590
around the world so that you can
place your servers so that they

00:39:34.590 --> 00:39:36.900
don't have the
internet between them

00:39:36.900 --> 00:39:41.787
and your services that
sit inside Google's Cloud.

00:39:41.787 --> 00:39:43.120
And we value the set of regions.

00:39:43.120 --> 00:39:46.960
We talked earlier about moving
into Germany and Frankfurt,

00:39:46.960 --> 00:39:49.750
and we're moving into other
areas around the world,

00:39:49.750 --> 00:39:51.790
expanding very, very fast.

00:39:51.790 --> 00:39:55.390
All of this is about giving
you unrivaled performance

00:39:55.390 --> 00:39:56.570
and scale.

00:39:56.570 --> 00:39:59.170
In fact, our investment
in delivering all of this

00:39:59.170 --> 00:40:01.510
is somewhere in the
region of $30 billion

00:40:01.510 --> 00:40:05.690
in terms of a trailing,
three year, CapEx investment.

00:40:05.690 --> 00:40:10.030
This is so that you don't
have to make that investment.

00:40:10.030 --> 00:40:13.540
We invest in the infrastructure
scalable, resilient,

00:40:13.540 --> 00:40:15.610
so that you don't have to do it.

00:40:15.610 --> 00:40:17.470
And we can deliver
it in a way that

00:40:17.470 --> 00:40:22.390
is very cost effective, and very
green, and very sustainable.

00:40:22.390 --> 00:40:25.570
We use technologies like
machine learning and AI

00:40:25.570 --> 00:40:27.940
to actually drive down
the energy consumption

00:40:27.940 --> 00:40:30.070
of our own data centers.

00:40:30.070 --> 00:40:32.980
Everything you see in the
services we offer outside we

00:40:32.980 --> 00:40:37.820
use inside, to enable Google
to scale and be reliable.

00:40:37.820 --> 00:40:40.450
And this is reflected in
our performance numbers.

00:40:40.450 --> 00:40:43.630
You will generally see that
Google is there or thereabouts

00:40:43.630 --> 00:40:46.600
in terms of providing the
highest performing as well

00:40:46.600 --> 00:40:51.760
as extremely reliable and
cost effective services.

00:40:51.760 --> 00:40:54.100
Finally, I'd like to
briefly talk about security

00:40:54.100 --> 00:40:56.890
because ultimately, what we
hear from our customers is

00:40:56.890 --> 00:40:59.530
all of this is great, but
without security, who really

00:40:59.530 --> 00:41:01.190
gives a damn about all of this.

00:41:01.190 --> 00:41:01.690
Right?

00:41:01.690 --> 00:41:03.690
It's just table stakes.

00:41:03.690 --> 00:41:07.760
At Google, it's everything
that we think about.

00:41:07.760 --> 00:41:10.520
Our entire stack is
optimized for security.

00:41:10.520 --> 00:41:12.530
We customize, we build
our own motherboards,

00:41:12.530 --> 00:41:16.220
we build our own chipsets, we
build our own operating systems

00:41:16.220 --> 00:41:18.310
so they can be
efficient, but also,

00:41:18.310 --> 00:41:21.740
so that we can get rid of all
of the unnecessary components

00:41:21.740 --> 00:41:25.860
so that people can't attack them
and they can't be compromised.

00:41:25.860 --> 00:41:28.370
So we end up with a
very, very secure stack.

00:41:28.370 --> 00:41:31.250
We even have our own
silicon, our own chips,

00:41:31.250 --> 00:41:34.760
that we put in our devices
to allow us to understand

00:41:34.760 --> 00:41:38.220
their entire history.

00:41:38.220 --> 00:41:39.930
In doing all of
this, that security

00:41:39.930 --> 00:41:42.190
also results in compliance.

00:41:42.190 --> 00:41:47.280
So with Google Cloud,
when we satisfy

00:41:47.280 --> 00:41:50.220
some criteria in terms of
compliance, for example,

00:41:50.220 --> 00:41:52.230
HIPAA in the United
States, or whatever,

00:41:52.230 --> 00:41:55.170
it doesn't apply to some
subset of Google Cloud.

00:41:55.170 --> 00:41:57.440
It's all of Google Cloud.

00:41:57.440 --> 00:42:00.330
And so all of Google Cloud
is essentially homogeneous.

00:42:00.330 --> 00:42:01.510
It looks the same.

00:42:01.510 --> 00:42:06.300
If it's qualified for some
criteria, then all of it is.

00:42:06.300 --> 00:42:11.050
And as Michael said earlier,
part of that compliance story

00:42:11.050 --> 00:42:13.150
is also around GDPR.

00:42:13.150 --> 00:42:18.170
And, you know, when it comes
to the fore in March next year,

00:42:18.170 --> 00:42:20.450
we're working very hard,
both with our customers

00:42:20.450 --> 00:42:24.720
and internally, to make
sure all of us are ready.

00:42:24.720 --> 00:42:29.500
A couple of quick areas
around security innovation.

00:42:29.500 --> 00:42:31.410
The first is
Identity Aware Proxy.

00:42:31.410 --> 00:42:33.252
Now, why would you
care about this?

00:42:33.252 --> 00:42:34.710
Well, Google's kind
of interesting.

00:42:34.710 --> 00:42:37.510
When I would work at Google,
when I joined Google,

00:42:37.510 --> 00:42:39.990
we actually don't
use VPNs to connect

00:42:39.990 --> 00:42:42.250
into Google infrastructure.

00:42:42.250 --> 00:42:45.130
We actually assume we
have a zero trust model,

00:42:45.130 --> 00:42:48.250
and what we want is fine grained
access to our applications

00:42:48.250 --> 00:42:50.050
and services inside Google.

00:42:50.050 --> 00:42:53.320
So what we do is we actually do
everything through a browser.

00:42:53.320 --> 00:42:56.560
It doesn't matter whether
we are on Google's Wi-Fi

00:42:56.560 --> 00:42:58.450
or outside on the internet.

00:42:58.450 --> 00:43:00.700
And this actually uses a
service, called the Identity

00:43:00.700 --> 00:43:04.360
Aware Proxy, that allows
us to get inside Google's

00:43:04.360 --> 00:43:07.000
infrastructure and to work
with all the applications

00:43:07.000 --> 00:43:09.010
and services we need
to, but with very

00:43:09.010 --> 00:43:10.720
fine grained security.

00:43:10.720 --> 00:43:14.800
And this we now offer as a
service for our customers.

00:43:14.800 --> 00:43:16.930
Secondly, another
area of innovation

00:43:16.930 --> 00:43:19.690
is the data loss prevention
API, which is really

00:43:19.690 --> 00:43:22.140
around focusing
on managing data,

00:43:22.140 --> 00:43:23.890
making sure that only
the right people can

00:43:23.890 --> 00:43:26.980
get at the right data,
and getting rid of data,

00:43:26.980 --> 00:43:29.260
or getting it out of
sight, for the people who

00:43:29.260 --> 00:43:31.170
shouldn't be at it.

00:43:31.170 --> 00:43:34.480
So what I'd like to do now
is to have a demonstration,

00:43:34.480 --> 00:43:36.190
and we'll talk about DLP.

00:43:36.190 --> 00:43:40.000
And to do that, please join
me in welcoming to the stage

00:43:40.000 --> 00:43:41.635
Lee Boonstra.

00:43:41.635 --> 00:43:44.605
[MUSIC PLAYING]

