You no longer have 20 years to
reconstruct new life science

00:58:48.240 --> 00:58:51.500
approaches, or think about
new transportation logistics

00:58:51.500 --> 00:58:55.110
solutions, or to rethink
digital communications

00:58:55.110 --> 00:58:56.250
across the globe.

00:58:56.250 --> 00:58:59.970
What you must do is you
must shrink that lifecycle.

00:58:59.970 --> 00:59:02.850
You must accelerate ideation.

00:59:02.850 --> 00:59:04.950
And the only way to
do that, we believe,

00:59:04.950 --> 00:59:09.150
is to be equipped with a
proper set of tools to do so.

00:59:09.150 --> 00:59:12.390
The Google Suite that
we have constructed,

00:59:12.390 --> 00:59:15.840
and the tools that we
would invite you to use,

00:59:15.840 --> 00:59:19.980
are built around this ability
to gather people together

00:59:19.980 --> 00:59:23.370
in real time to
collaborate to come up

00:59:23.370 --> 00:59:25.410
with the big idea,
which is going

00:59:25.410 --> 00:59:29.470
to lead to the transformation
that you're looking to do.

00:59:29.470 --> 00:59:32.910
So we have a set of tools that
allow you to create and to rise

00:59:32.910 --> 00:59:34.740
that level of creation
instead of being

00:59:34.740 --> 00:59:38.760
an individual activity, but to
make it a group activity where

00:59:38.760 --> 00:59:41.020
you're working in real time.

00:59:41.020 --> 00:59:43.330
We provide tools that
allow you to connect.

00:59:43.330 --> 00:59:46.360
Connect in real
time, exchange ideas,

00:59:46.360 --> 00:59:48.670
and be able to
riff off each other

00:59:48.670 --> 00:59:51.670
and synthesize new
thoughts in new areas.

00:59:51.670 --> 00:59:55.000
We do that with the ability
to quickly search and access

00:59:55.000 --> 00:59:58.427
content in one place,
held in Google Drive,

00:59:58.427 --> 01:00:00.010
and then of course
we have to give you

01:00:00.010 --> 01:00:04.060
the control, the security,
the compliance, the DLP

01:00:04.060 --> 01:00:06.940
capabilities, to be able
to protect that content so

01:00:06.940 --> 01:00:10.240
that you can be compliant
with things like GDPR.

01:00:12.760 --> 01:00:15.510
And when you do it,
there's an impact.

01:00:15.510 --> 01:00:18.210
PWC adopted the
Google Apps or G Suite

01:00:18.210 --> 01:00:20.100
platform several years ago.

01:00:20.100 --> 01:00:24.240
They rolled it out worldwide,
and they saw a dramatic impact

01:00:24.240 --> 01:00:26.350
in terms of their productivity.

01:00:26.350 --> 01:00:28.670
If you think about the
world that they live in,

01:00:28.670 --> 01:00:32.376
their consultants are working
every day, both internally

01:00:32.376 --> 01:00:33.750
and externally,
to be able to try

01:00:33.750 --> 01:00:38.910
to advise on financial issues,
tax issues, and a variety

01:00:38.910 --> 01:00:40.770
of other consulting areas.

01:00:40.770 --> 01:00:43.020
What they have found
is that by being

01:00:43.020 --> 01:00:44.940
able to reduce the
amount of travel

01:00:44.940 --> 01:00:46.380
they have through
Google Hangouts

01:00:46.380 --> 01:00:48.180
and being able to
connect real time,

01:00:48.180 --> 01:00:50.250
through collaborative
document editing

01:00:50.250 --> 01:00:53.590
and inviting both the
customer and the suppliers

01:00:53.590 --> 01:00:55.800
that they're involved with
into the conversation,

01:00:55.800 --> 01:00:59.280
they're finding that they're
able to get things done faster.

01:00:59.280 --> 01:01:02.100
And the ability to be able
to connect in real time,

01:01:02.100 --> 01:01:04.740
those things have
led to real savings.

01:01:04.740 --> 01:01:06.540
In a study that they
did, they came back

01:01:06.540 --> 01:01:08.220
and said that on
average, they are

01:01:08.220 --> 01:01:11.220
saving nine hours per
week per employee,

01:01:11.220 --> 01:01:12.720
and they have a
lot of employees.

01:01:12.720 --> 01:01:13.800
That's real numbers.

01:01:13.800 --> 01:01:16.420
And the financial people
love the impact of that.

01:01:19.040 --> 01:01:21.290
But why can't we
just use the tools

01:01:21.290 --> 01:01:25.170
we've been using for years
to accomplish the same thing?

01:01:25.170 --> 01:01:28.080
Our argument and
our platform says

01:01:28.080 --> 01:01:30.780
that the way of collaboration
that our competitors are

01:01:30.780 --> 01:01:33.390
offering is really an old
school style of methods.

01:01:33.390 --> 01:01:35.190
They haven't really
changed, fundamentally,

01:01:35.190 --> 01:01:36.460
the way you work.

01:01:36.460 --> 01:01:40.410
In fact, I often talk to
C level executives that

01:01:40.410 --> 01:01:42.270
have adopted the
competitor platform,

01:01:42.270 --> 01:01:44.490
and I've asked them
this one question.

01:01:44.490 --> 01:01:46.590
What are you doing
different today

01:01:46.590 --> 01:01:49.650
now that you've adopted the
latest from your competitor,

01:01:49.650 --> 01:01:52.320
and how has that transformed
the way you work, and how has

01:01:52.320 --> 01:01:55.584
that accelerated your
ideation platform?

01:01:55.584 --> 01:01:57.000
And in every case,
they say, well,

01:01:57.000 --> 01:01:59.610
Bill, that's not
what we were about.

01:01:59.610 --> 01:02:03.630
We wanted to eliminate the
impact of change management

01:02:03.630 --> 01:02:04.530
on our organization.

01:02:04.530 --> 01:02:06.540
We didn't really want
to change anything.

01:02:06.540 --> 01:02:08.666
We liked the way
we were working.

01:02:08.666 --> 01:02:09.540
And I'm like, really?

01:02:09.540 --> 01:02:11.010
Is that what you want to do?

01:02:11.010 --> 01:02:12.510
You want to just
work the way you've

01:02:12.510 --> 01:02:15.390
been working for 20 years,
where an individual creates

01:02:15.390 --> 01:02:18.870
a document, or a spreadsheet,
or a presentation,

01:02:18.870 --> 01:02:21.030
and then they send
it out in email

01:02:21.030 --> 01:02:23.400
as an attachment as
a way to collaborate

01:02:23.400 --> 01:02:25.650
and be able to exchange ideas?

01:02:25.650 --> 01:02:29.700
If you were going to come up
with the top 10 vacation list

01:02:29.700 --> 01:02:34.230
ideas in a matter of minutes,
would you get into a document

01:02:34.230 --> 01:02:36.120
and list the top 10
and then send it out

01:02:36.120 --> 01:02:38.340
to 10 of your colleagues
and wait for them to reply?

01:02:38.340 --> 01:02:38.970
No.

01:02:38.970 --> 01:02:42.120
You would actively engage with
them in real time, most likely

01:02:42.120 --> 01:02:44.160
in a conference room
or in a Google Doc,

01:02:44.160 --> 01:02:46.470
and say, let's come up
with the top 10 quickly.

01:02:46.470 --> 01:02:49.590
And when everybody is
typing at the same time

01:02:49.590 --> 01:02:53.310
and they're all interacting in a
collaborative way, what happens

01:02:53.310 --> 01:02:56.100
is you begin to raise
the ideas quickly.

01:02:56.100 --> 01:02:59.580
You begin to collaborate and
have visual inspection of what

01:02:59.580 --> 01:03:00.840
other people are doing.

01:03:00.840 --> 01:03:02.430
And now what you
can do is you can

01:03:02.430 --> 01:03:06.840
avoid the need to replicate
what Greg has put down.

01:03:06.840 --> 01:03:08.280
So Greg and I are collaborating.

01:03:08.280 --> 01:03:09.960
Tom and I are collaborating.

01:03:09.960 --> 01:03:11.860
Alexander and I
are collaborating.

01:03:11.860 --> 01:03:14.940
And I can quickly see they
don't like Cancun, Mexico.

01:03:14.940 --> 01:03:19.761
They prefer Nice or Malaga
Spain to be able to do vacation.

01:03:19.761 --> 01:03:20.760
That's their preference.

01:03:20.760 --> 01:03:23.010
They're Europeans, and I
don't have that experience.

01:03:23.010 --> 01:03:24.300
Now, I do live in
London now, which

01:03:24.300 --> 01:03:25.650
has been a really eye
opening experience

01:03:25.650 --> 01:03:26.775
and I'm really enjoying it.

01:03:29.130 --> 01:03:29.850
All right.

01:03:29.850 --> 01:03:31.560
And then the most
important thing

01:03:31.560 --> 01:03:33.150
that I really love
about the Suite

01:03:33.150 --> 01:03:35.830
is the ability to
connect in real time.

01:03:35.830 --> 01:03:38.790
The reality is
that we don't have

01:03:38.790 --> 01:03:41.550
to wait for the Thursday
afternoon meeting that's

01:03:41.550 --> 01:03:44.520
scheduled for one hour because
all meetings must go an hour.

01:03:44.520 --> 01:03:46.200
That's just the
rule of meetings.

01:03:46.200 --> 01:03:46.770
Right?

01:03:46.770 --> 01:03:48.430
That's not the case.

01:03:48.430 --> 01:03:50.790
When I can have an
idea or an inspiration

01:03:50.790 --> 01:03:53.760
and I can literally reach
out to Joe and Karen and say,

01:03:53.760 --> 01:03:55.880
can you join me on a
Hangout for five minutes

01:03:55.880 --> 01:03:58.442
and let's walk through
this concept and this idea.

01:03:58.442 --> 01:04:00.900
And when I ping them and they
come back to me and they say,

01:04:00.900 --> 01:04:03.030
yep, I'm available, and
we get on a Hangout,

01:04:03.030 --> 01:04:06.930
in five or 10 minutes, we can
move the idea forward in a way

01:04:06.930 --> 01:04:09.990
that a meeting on Thursday
afternoon at 3 o'clock

01:04:09.990 --> 01:04:11.500
would not do.

01:04:11.500 --> 01:04:13.890
So the ability to
instantaneously reach out,

01:04:13.890 --> 01:04:16.170
click of a button, and
connect in real time

01:04:16.170 --> 01:04:18.100
is a powerful capability.

01:04:18.100 --> 01:04:20.615
And that's what we're doing.

01:04:20.615 --> 01:04:21.990
And then lastly,
and you're going

01:04:21.990 --> 01:04:25.230
to hear a lot more about
this, is the application

01:04:25.230 --> 01:04:28.230
of artificial intelligence
to this environment.

01:04:28.230 --> 01:04:30.570
AI, in its simplest
form, is really

01:04:30.570 --> 01:04:33.817
about observing
patterns and then

01:04:33.817 --> 01:04:36.150
being able to recognize the
outcomes that are associated

01:04:36.150 --> 01:04:38.430
those patterns, and then
being able to do something

01:04:38.430 --> 01:04:41.070
that's predictive and being
able to actually look forward.

01:04:41.070 --> 01:04:42.990
I think of it like this.

01:04:42.990 --> 01:04:46.530
If I asked you to be a diving
judge at the next Olympics,

01:04:46.530 --> 01:04:48.510
how qualified are you today?

01:04:48.510 --> 01:04:50.130
Probably not very
well qualified.

01:04:50.130 --> 01:04:51.330
I'm not.

01:04:51.330 --> 01:04:54.090
But if you sat with a
Olympic judge expert

01:04:54.090 --> 01:04:56.860
and watched 100 dives, you'd
be a little bit better.

01:04:56.860 --> 01:04:58.800
What if you watched 1,000 dives?

01:04:58.800 --> 01:04:59.830
10,000 dives?

01:04:59.830 --> 01:05:00.930
100,000 dives?

01:05:00.930 --> 01:05:02.730
A million dives?

01:05:02.730 --> 01:05:06.660
We are literally taking samples
from a billion people that

01:05:06.660 --> 01:05:08.967
are simultaneously
using our products,

01:05:08.967 --> 01:05:10.800
and we're able to look
at what they're doing

01:05:10.800 --> 01:05:12.390
and predict what's next.

01:05:12.390 --> 01:05:13.980
And one of the great
examples of that

01:05:13.980 --> 01:05:18.780
is expanded upon in
Explore in Sheets.

01:05:18.780 --> 01:05:21.880
If you regularly get
a dataset, maybe it's

01:05:21.880 --> 01:05:24.060
a point of sale data,
and you're trying

01:05:24.060 --> 01:05:26.730
to ask a simple question
like, OK, Google.

01:05:26.730 --> 01:05:31.080
Show me the highest
profit product that

01:05:31.080 --> 01:05:34.050
was sold during the months
of September, October,

01:05:34.050 --> 01:05:37.830
and November across the
different states in Germany,

01:05:37.830 --> 01:05:40.320
and illustrate--

01:05:40.320 --> 01:05:42.270
raise to the top
the most productive

01:05:42.270 --> 01:05:46.500
or the best margin product
during that period.

01:05:46.500 --> 01:05:49.110
You don't want to have to write
the formula that does that.

01:05:49.110 --> 01:05:53.120
But Google has been watching
the way experts analyze data.

01:05:53.120 --> 01:05:57.000
And from thousands and
millions of examples,

01:05:57.000 --> 01:05:59.820
they can actually-- we can
actually predict what's next

01:05:59.820 --> 01:06:03.120
and offer some insight,
immediately, when you open up.

01:06:03.120 --> 01:06:05.430
So all you have to do is
hit the Explore button,

01:06:05.430 --> 01:06:08.790
and now I have a set of
graphs, and charts, and pivots,

01:06:08.790 --> 01:06:12.240
that are already available
to me to be able to examine.

01:06:12.240 --> 01:06:14.760
And I can do that without having
to do any work whatsoever,

01:06:14.760 --> 01:06:16.530
and then I can start
to ask questions,

01:06:16.530 --> 01:06:19.650
and Google will actually
write the formula for you.

01:06:19.650 --> 01:06:21.330
So we're doing a
lot of work in AI,

01:06:21.330 --> 01:06:23.574
and that's only one
example of many,

01:06:23.574 --> 01:06:24.990
in terms of what
we're doing to be

01:06:24.990 --> 01:06:27.870
able to app apply
artificial intelligence

01:06:27.870 --> 01:06:32.790
and machine learning to your
work as you collaborate.

01:06:32.790 --> 01:06:35.060
So let's not just
take my word for it.

01:06:35.060 --> 01:06:37.470
But what I'd like to
do is invite a customer

01:06:37.470 --> 01:06:39.960
to come up and talk a little
bit about what they're doing

01:06:39.960 --> 01:06:41.370
in the application at G Suite.

01:06:41.370 --> 01:06:44.310
So I'm happy to invite
Alex Pollmann from Vismon

01:06:44.310 --> 01:06:46.688
to come and share
his experience.

01:06:46.688 --> 01:06:49.183
[MUSIC PLAYING]

01:06:55.180 --> 01:06:56.790
ALEXANDER POLLMANN:
So yeah, welcome.

01:06:56.790 --> 01:06:58.190
Good morning from my side.

01:06:58.190 --> 01:07:00.730
I'm really happy, proud
to tell you, that we

01:07:00.730 --> 01:07:02.310
have gone Google this year.

01:07:02.310 --> 01:07:05.590
And we have gone G Suite.

01:07:05.590 --> 01:07:09.630
It's a digital transformation
at Vismon with G Suite.

01:07:09.630 --> 01:07:12.580
Vismon is a traditional
family owned business,

01:07:12.580 --> 01:07:14.590
and we are producing
heating boilers,

01:07:14.590 --> 01:07:19.360
cooling systems, and also
some industrial systems.

01:07:19.360 --> 01:07:22.600
Our revenue is about
2.2 billion euros,

01:07:22.600 --> 01:07:26.390
and we have 12,000 employees
all over the world.

01:07:26.390 --> 01:07:31.570
So we started our digital
transformation around 2015

01:07:31.570 --> 01:07:35.200
because we saw their new
business models came up,

01:07:35.200 --> 01:07:38.980
new competitors, also
company of alphabet,

01:07:38.980 --> 01:07:41.320
Nest I have also on my slides.

01:07:41.320 --> 01:07:42.730
They came up, and
they are taking

01:07:42.730 --> 01:07:48.740
our core business, and also our
thoughts about new businesses.

01:07:48.740 --> 01:07:52.130
So what we have done, we
have spread out a strategy

01:07:52.130 --> 01:07:53.720
for our digital transformation.

01:07:53.720 --> 01:07:57.530
And there was a project
called the future workplace.

01:07:57.530 --> 01:08:00.620
So the future workplace
is more about--

01:08:00.620 --> 01:08:05.000
at the very beginning, it's more
about evaluation of a platform

01:08:05.000 --> 01:08:05.640
solution.

01:08:05.640 --> 01:08:10.220
So we came from an IBM
Notes infrastructure.

01:08:10.220 --> 01:08:13.950
We have run IBM Notes
for these 22 years,

01:08:13.950 --> 01:08:18.660
and you know it is a big
growing infrastructure.

01:08:18.660 --> 01:08:22.640
So our priorities also
lay on the usability.

01:08:22.640 --> 01:08:25.189
Because if you want
to use your software

01:08:25.189 --> 01:08:27.630
and you'll also want
to use a user software,

01:08:27.630 --> 01:08:32.210
you have to get them
on an easy software.

01:08:32.210 --> 01:08:34.029
Functionality was
also an [INAUDIBLE]..

01:08:34.029 --> 01:08:35.750
Also, the IT security.

01:08:35.750 --> 01:08:38.990
All of you heard about the
security layer of Google.

01:08:38.990 --> 01:08:42.380
This is maybe also one of the
reasons why we choose Google

01:08:42.380 --> 01:08:48.029
because we think it is the most
secure platform for us to use.

01:08:48.029 --> 01:08:50.750
So the integration part,
and also the costs,

01:08:50.750 --> 01:08:53.359
were also prioritized
in the evaluation.

01:08:53.359 --> 01:08:56.000
At the end, we decided
to move to G Suite

01:08:56.000 --> 01:08:57.590
and we are really happy.

01:08:57.590 --> 01:09:00.770
We have now workplace
that is easily

01:09:00.770 --> 01:09:04.490
accessible on any device,
at any place, at any time.

01:09:04.490 --> 01:09:05.990
Paul mentioned,
our people should

01:09:05.990 --> 01:09:09.080
engage any time, any place.

01:09:09.080 --> 01:09:11.779
They should easily get together.

01:09:11.779 --> 01:09:13.850
They should create ideas.

01:09:13.850 --> 01:09:16.859
We have now more flexibility,
more security, more

01:09:16.859 --> 01:09:19.970
a team collaboration,
and also more

01:09:19.970 --> 01:09:22.265
transparency at the same costs.

01:09:24.890 --> 01:09:26.990
So our main
achievements, like, we

01:09:26.990 --> 01:09:31.109
have started the global rollout
for G Suite this year, in June.

01:09:31.109 --> 01:09:34.760
And we have an average
1,500 people each month

01:09:34.760 --> 01:09:36.350
coming to G Suite.

01:09:36.350 --> 01:09:38.410
We have migrated them.

01:09:38.410 --> 01:09:43.330
15% of our users are using
bring your own device services.

01:09:43.330 --> 01:09:48.069
Just imagine if,
like, 1,200 people

01:09:48.069 --> 01:09:51.430
spent 10 minutes each
day in their private life

01:09:51.430 --> 01:09:53.750
with their work environment.

01:09:53.750 --> 01:09:59.290
That are, like, 200 hours a
day your company will save.

01:09:59.290 --> 01:10:03.730
1.7 million emails doesn't
need to be archived anymore.

01:10:03.730 --> 01:10:07.150
In IBM Notes, we had
just 100 megabyte space

01:10:07.150 --> 01:10:09.580
left in your email.

01:10:09.580 --> 01:10:11.140
No other storage is unlimited.

01:10:11.140 --> 01:10:13.060
It's great.

01:10:13.060 --> 01:10:15.720
And everyone uses two
factor identification.

01:10:15.720 --> 01:10:20.930
So everyone can reach his
work benefits from everywhere.

01:10:20.930 --> 01:10:24.920
And he just needs to get
his smartphone [INAUDIBLE]

01:10:24.920 --> 01:10:28.610
and say, yes, I am.

01:10:28.610 --> 01:10:32.320
So 4,500 Google Meets
are done each month.

01:10:32.320 --> 01:10:35.890
So if you've heard,
it's just six months ago

01:10:35.890 --> 01:10:39.800
we started the rollout, and it's
pretty increasing, this number.

01:10:39.800 --> 01:10:42.090
Also, it's, like, 4,500.

01:10:42.090 --> 01:10:49.260
If you think for 4,500, if
for one Google Meet of 100

01:10:49.260 --> 01:10:52.370
is just a business trip,
you can save as a company.

01:10:52.370 --> 01:10:57.000
You will save around 45 to 50
business trips every month,

01:10:57.000 --> 01:10:59.260
and your people could engage
everywhere, everywhere,

01:10:59.260 --> 01:11:01.240
at any time.

01:11:01.240 --> 01:11:04.050
So the last part is all
about collaboration.

01:11:04.050 --> 01:11:07.250
We don't send any
documents anymore.

01:11:07.250 --> 01:11:10.530
It's all, like, we share
it, and collaborate in one

01:11:10.530 --> 01:11:12.930
document and all this stuff.

01:11:12.930 --> 01:11:13.940
I really like it.

01:11:13.940 --> 01:11:16.860
And I really would
like to thank all

01:11:16.860 --> 01:11:19.940
of my team colleagues,
and also our partner,

01:11:19.940 --> 01:11:21.670
[INAUDIBLE],, for
the great support

01:11:21.670 --> 01:11:24.030
we had during the migration.

01:11:24.030 --> 01:11:28.800
But I'm pretty sure
that this is just

01:11:28.800 --> 01:11:32.970
the beginning of a big change
at our future workplace.

01:11:32.970 --> 01:11:39.030
And I'm really looking forward
to all the new cool stuff

01:11:39.030 --> 01:11:41.160
that will come from
Google in the future.

01:11:41.160 --> 01:11:43.140
Thank you, guys.

01:11:43.140 --> 01:11:46.110
[APPLAUSE]

01:11:49.072 --> 01:11:51.030
BILL HIPPENMEYER: Thank
you, Alex, for sharing.

01:11:51.030 --> 01:11:52.440
I really appreciate that.

01:11:52.440 --> 01:11:55.470
So we've talked about
optimize, about leveraging

01:11:55.470 --> 01:11:57.510
the power of Google
infrastructure, network,

01:11:57.510 --> 01:11:59.165
and data center services.

01:11:59.165 --> 01:12:00.540
Hopefully you've
got some insight

01:12:00.540 --> 01:12:03.180
into why collaboration
is so important to us.

01:12:03.180 --> 01:12:05.550
What I'd like to
do next is take you

01:12:05.550 --> 01:12:08.370
to the next level,
which is accelerate.

01:12:08.370 --> 01:12:12.090
In acceleration, it's really
about learning and growing,

01:12:12.090 --> 01:12:15.270
and harnessing the power of
big data, machine learning,

01:12:15.270 --> 01:12:17.460
and technologies like
that, that will really

01:12:17.460 --> 01:12:21.015
help you be able to move
faster, and to move better.

01:12:21.015 --> 01:12:22.890
So with that, I'd like
to introduce my friend

01:12:22.890 --> 01:12:24.930
and colleague, Greg
DeMichillie, who's

01:12:24.930 --> 01:12:26.791
going to talk a little
bit about accelerate.

01:12:26.791 --> 01:12:29.677
[MUSIC PLAYING]

01:12:34.020 --> 01:12:35.816
GREG DEMICHILLIE:
Thanks, everyone.

01:12:35.816 --> 01:12:38.190
I'm really excited to talk
about this section of the talk

01:12:38.190 --> 01:12:42.990
because, as Paul said, going
into Cloud to just shift

01:12:42.990 --> 01:12:46.740
a little bit of CapEx to
OpEx or save 5 or 10%,

01:12:46.740 --> 01:12:49.860
really misses the opportunity
that it provides you.

01:12:49.860 --> 01:12:52.320
The opportunity to
not just save money,

01:12:52.320 --> 01:12:54.900
not just to affect your
cost side of the business,

01:12:54.900 --> 01:12:57.010
but to drive new business.

01:12:57.010 --> 01:12:59.310
And the best way
to think about that

01:12:59.310 --> 01:13:01.050
is to think about
it in terms of,

01:13:01.050 --> 01:13:04.750
how do you get
value out of data?

01:13:04.750 --> 01:13:05.880
Think about it.

01:13:05.880 --> 01:13:09.120
As companies, we have
become digital pack rats.

01:13:09.120 --> 01:13:12.510
We store ever increasing
amounts of data.

01:13:12.510 --> 01:13:16.320
By some estimates, we'll have
163 zettabytes of data per year

01:13:16.320 --> 01:13:17.820
by 2025.

01:13:17.820 --> 01:13:20.620
I had to Google what a
zettabyte is, by the way.

01:13:20.620 --> 01:13:22.470
Just in case you
don't know, I believe

01:13:22.470 --> 01:13:24.750
it's a trillion gigabytes.

01:13:24.750 --> 01:13:27.210
And why is it that
we're doing that?

01:13:27.210 --> 01:13:30.120
The Cloud has made it possible.

01:13:30.120 --> 01:13:32.760
To store a petabyte
of data 10 years ago,

01:13:32.760 --> 01:13:34.620
you had to get a
robotic tape archive.

01:13:34.620 --> 01:13:38.230
You had to spend millions of
dollars on special machinery.

01:13:38.230 --> 01:13:41.100
Now, for a penny a
gigabyte a month,

01:13:41.100 --> 01:13:44.070
you can store every bit
of data from your factory,

01:13:44.070 --> 01:13:47.460
or your business, or
your retail operation.

01:13:47.460 --> 01:13:49.560
So the problem has
shifted from, how

01:13:49.560 --> 01:13:54.690
do I store that data, to how
do I find the needle of value

01:13:54.690 --> 01:13:57.570
in that haystack of data?

01:13:57.570 --> 01:14:00.161
Now the way this works for
most of us today is big data.

01:14:00.161 --> 01:14:00.660
Right?

01:14:00.660 --> 01:14:01.530
That's the answer.

01:14:01.530 --> 01:14:02.550
Big data.

01:14:02.550 --> 01:14:05.430
And we know something
about that at Google,

01:14:05.430 --> 01:14:09.000
because most of what
is in big data today

01:14:09.000 --> 01:14:13.050
dates to research papers that
Google published 10 years ago.

01:14:13.050 --> 01:14:15.630
Big data is built on
a foundation of work

01:14:15.630 --> 01:14:18.510
that we did within Google.

01:14:18.510 --> 01:14:20.610
And we went through
this pattern which

01:14:20.610 --> 01:14:23.130
is we build these
big data systems,

01:14:23.130 --> 01:14:27.180
we run them in production, the
data grows by a factor of 10,

01:14:27.180 --> 01:14:29.790
we have to re-architect
the storage system,

01:14:29.790 --> 01:14:31.920
and the result is, if
you look at most data

01:14:31.920 --> 01:14:34.380
big data projects within
enterprises today,

01:14:34.380 --> 01:14:37.740
they're not achieving the
results that they promised.

01:14:37.740 --> 01:14:38.910
And why is it?

01:14:38.910 --> 01:14:40.950
It's because if you look
at where you actually

01:14:40.950 --> 01:14:43.080
spend your dollars
and your time,

01:14:43.080 --> 01:14:45.060
it's not on getting insights.

01:14:45.060 --> 01:14:50.830
You spend way too much time on
building maintenance, logging,

01:14:50.830 --> 01:14:54.180
backup, disaster
recovery, oh no, the data

01:14:54.180 --> 01:14:57.150
is 10 times as big, let me
rebuild my sharding system.

01:14:57.150 --> 01:15:00.690
Nobody enjoys doing that, yet
we do it time and time again.

01:15:00.690 --> 01:15:04.440
You spend too much time
taking care of the machinery,

01:15:04.440 --> 01:15:08.260
and not enough time actually
understanding the data.

01:15:08.260 --> 01:15:10.590
And again, we know this
is the case because we

01:15:10.590 --> 01:15:12.120
went through this at Google.

01:15:12.120 --> 01:15:14.850
We went through this
exact same pattern.

01:15:14.850 --> 01:15:18.000
We built data systems, we
thought they were great,

01:15:18.000 --> 01:15:20.590
the data got too big,
we had to rebuild them,

01:15:20.590 --> 01:15:22.710
and we realized we had
to have a better way.

01:15:22.710 --> 01:15:26.610
We had to have a different
way to think about big data.

01:15:26.610 --> 01:15:29.820
So we set about building out
an entirely new set of data

01:15:29.820 --> 01:15:33.600
and analytics products around
the simple idea of reversing

01:15:33.600 --> 01:15:37.770
this ratio, of inverting the
amount of time you spent taking

01:15:37.770 --> 01:15:40.020
care of the system
and the amount of time

01:15:40.020 --> 01:15:42.650
you actually spent
getting Insights.

01:15:42.650 --> 01:15:45.150
So across our entire
data portfolio,

01:15:45.150 --> 01:15:49.290
you will see products built
natively for the Cloud

01:15:49.290 --> 01:15:53.040
where all of the work of
scaling, and infrastructure,

01:15:53.040 --> 01:15:58.310
and management, and even DBA,
is taken care of for you.

01:15:58.310 --> 01:16:00.290
And we've also gone
one step further,

01:16:00.290 --> 01:16:03.410
because we also realized at
Google, that traditional batch

01:16:03.410 --> 01:16:05.660
analytics aren't good enough.

01:16:05.660 --> 01:16:09.290
The days when you could have a
weekly report of your business

01:16:09.290 --> 01:16:12.860
and call that acceptable,
if you're a retailer,

01:16:12.860 --> 01:16:14.580
you want to know
what's happening today,

01:16:14.580 --> 01:16:16.970
not what happened two weeks ago.

01:16:16.970 --> 01:16:18.654
If you're a
manufacturer, you want

01:16:18.654 --> 01:16:20.570
to know that you're
having production problems

01:16:20.570 --> 01:16:23.510
today, not on a quarterly
report when it's too late

01:16:23.510 --> 01:16:25.462
to do anything about it.

01:16:25.462 --> 01:16:27.170
So we built our systems
around the notion

01:16:27.170 --> 01:16:30.200
that batch and streaming
are the same thing.

01:16:30.200 --> 01:16:33.110
That it should be as easy
to get real time insights

01:16:33.110 --> 01:16:36.740
as it is to get a daily, or
weekly, or quarterly business

01:16:36.740 --> 01:16:38.640
report.

01:16:38.640 --> 01:16:41.670
And so when you look across
our entire data portfolio,

01:16:41.670 --> 01:16:44.550
whether it's BigQuery, which
is a data and analytics

01:16:44.550 --> 01:16:46.560
warehouse in the
Cloud-- literally,

01:16:46.560 --> 01:16:49.620
upload a petabyte of
data, write a SQL query.

01:16:49.620 --> 01:16:50.550
Done.

01:16:50.550 --> 01:16:55.280
No DBA, no sharding,
nothing to set up.

01:16:55.280 --> 01:16:58.700
Or Dataproc, which lets you take
your existing Hadoop clusters,

01:16:58.700 --> 01:17:00.470
bring them in the
Cloud, and get out

01:17:00.470 --> 01:17:04.930
of the business of managing
the Hadoop infrastructure.

01:17:04.930 --> 01:17:07.630
Or Pub/Sub and Dataflow,
which you heard about

01:17:07.630 --> 01:17:11.350
from our customers, using that
to transform the way you ingest

01:17:11.350 --> 01:17:12.880
data in real time.

01:17:12.880 --> 01:17:15.460
What all these products
have in common is

01:17:15.460 --> 01:17:19.540
they get you out of the drudgery
of maintaining the machinery.

01:17:19.540 --> 01:17:21.910
And they let you
focus on the fun part.

01:17:21.910 --> 01:17:23.980
I know of no data
analytics professional

01:17:23.980 --> 01:17:28.270
who got into it because they
liked writing sharding systems.

01:17:28.270 --> 01:17:30.550
Do what's the fun
part, the understanding

01:17:30.550 --> 01:17:33.020
the analytics of data.

01:17:33.020 --> 01:17:36.390
And we see this having
an impact on customers.

01:17:36.390 --> 01:17:37.972
Spotify, when they
joined Google,

01:17:37.972 --> 01:17:39.930
if you talk to their
execs, they will tell you,

01:17:39.930 --> 01:17:43.200
the number one reason that
they wanted to use Google

01:17:43.200 --> 01:17:46.120
was to give them a
competitive advantage in data.

01:17:46.120 --> 01:17:47.100
Think about it.

01:17:47.100 --> 01:17:50.550
Every time you skip a
song, or you play a song,

01:17:50.550 --> 01:17:52.500
or use search for a
song in Spotify, that's

01:17:52.500 --> 01:17:56.100
data that helps them predict
that next perfect music

01:17:56.100 --> 01:17:57.930
for you.

01:17:57.930 --> 01:18:00.300
Or in the banking
industry, Lloyd's,

01:18:00.300 --> 01:18:03.420
where they say they
literally went from 96 hours

01:18:03.420 --> 01:18:06.060
to 30 minutes in
terms of their ability

01:18:06.060 --> 01:18:08.400
to go from an event
happening in the market

01:18:08.400 --> 01:18:13.140
to them being able to make
a decision based on that.

01:18:13.140 --> 01:18:15.230
Now, what's the
next step of this?

01:18:15.230 --> 01:18:16.910
Sure, we can build
data warehouses.

01:18:16.910 --> 01:18:18.410
You've heard mentioned
several times

01:18:18.410 --> 01:18:22.390
this notion of machine learning
and artificial intelligence.

01:18:22.390 --> 01:18:25.120
And we really think
that is the next step.

01:18:25.120 --> 01:18:30.320
It's the next evolution in
how you do data analytics.

01:18:30.320 --> 01:18:32.240
And to prove just how
much we believe in it,

01:18:32.240 --> 01:18:34.910
our CEO, Sundar Pichai,
has said that this

01:18:34.910 --> 01:18:37.490
is a core transformative
way where we're

01:18:37.490 --> 01:18:40.010
rethinking everything
we're doing

01:18:40.010 --> 01:18:41.810
and that is literally
true at Google.

01:18:41.810 --> 01:18:44.390
There is not a project or
a product inside Google

01:18:44.390 --> 01:18:46.970
that isn't being
rethought in terms

01:18:46.970 --> 01:18:50.250
of machine learning and
artificial intelligence.

01:18:50.250 --> 01:18:51.870
If you think about
the consumer side,

01:18:51.870 --> 01:18:55.020
every way that you interact with
Google now has an AI or machine

01:18:55.020 --> 01:18:58.200
learning component,
whether it's your Android

01:18:58.200 --> 01:19:01.410
smartphone and the way it
does speech recognition

01:19:01.410 --> 01:19:03.720
or auto correction.

01:19:03.720 --> 01:19:05.580
Or how many of you
have used Google Photos

01:19:05.580 --> 01:19:09.090
and typed birthday
party clown and found

01:19:09.090 --> 01:19:12.090
a picture from three years ago
of your child's birthday party

01:19:12.090 --> 01:19:14.250
when you had a clown come in?

01:19:14.250 --> 01:19:17.250
Or when you've used Gmail,
as Bill talked about,

01:19:17.250 --> 01:19:20.070
where you've had an
automatic reply generated.

01:19:20.070 --> 01:19:21.810
All of these are
built on the notion

01:19:21.810 --> 01:19:24.240
of artificial intelligence
and machine learning.

01:19:24.240 --> 01:19:26.400
Now it's reasonable to ask, why?

01:19:26.400 --> 01:19:28.710
I mean, Google has
smart programmers.

01:19:28.710 --> 01:19:33.210
Couldn't we have just done this
ourselves without using ML?

01:19:33.210 --> 01:19:35.280
And I think it's important
to think about why

01:19:35.280 --> 01:19:37.820
ML was so transformative.

01:19:37.820 --> 01:19:43.140
ML lets us solve problems that
we don't know how to solve.

01:19:43.140 --> 01:19:46.600
Think about when you
learned your first language.

01:19:46.600 --> 01:19:50.010
Unless you have fairly
unusual parents,

01:19:50.010 --> 01:19:54.130
I don't think they handed you a
grammar book and a dictionary.

01:19:54.130 --> 01:19:56.050
But that's what we
do as programmers.

01:19:56.050 --> 01:19:59.590
We teach our computers with
grammar books and dictionaries.

01:19:59.590 --> 01:20:01.300
You learned your first
language by being

01:20:01.300 --> 01:20:04.060
exposed to it, by example.

01:20:04.060 --> 01:20:05.680
Your family had a dog.

01:20:05.680 --> 01:20:07.360
Your parents said dog.

01:20:07.360 --> 01:20:08.860
You saw the dog.

01:20:08.860 --> 01:20:11.620
Literally, the
chemistry in your brain

01:20:11.620 --> 01:20:14.710
changed, and you learned
that that piece--

01:20:14.710 --> 01:20:16.900
that animal was a dog.

01:20:16.900 --> 01:20:19.660
ML allows us to take
that same technique.

01:20:19.660 --> 01:20:22.840
Learn by example, not
by rote, and apply it

01:20:22.840 --> 01:20:24.350
to business problems.

01:20:24.350 --> 01:20:28.150
Think about your factories, your
customer data, your sales data,

01:20:28.150 --> 01:20:31.330
where there is hidden value
and connections in your data

01:20:31.330 --> 01:20:33.460
that you're not
taking advantage of.

01:20:33.460 --> 01:20:36.810
ML and AI lets us do that.

01:20:36.810 --> 01:20:40.400
So we've built a complete
platform around AI and ML.

01:20:40.400 --> 01:20:42.800
At the bottom is all
that same infrastructure

01:20:42.800 --> 01:20:44.240
that you heard about earlier.

01:20:44.240 --> 01:20:46.130
The network, the
compute, the storage.

01:20:46.130 --> 01:20:48.320
Because at the
end of the day, ML

01:20:48.320 --> 01:20:51.970
is a real big data
and compute problem.

01:20:51.970 --> 01:20:54.400
On top of that, we build
platforms, libraries,

01:20:54.400 --> 01:20:58.210
and tools, tools that let
you take your business data

01:20:58.210 --> 01:21:01.450
and train your own models
without having to worry about

01:21:01.450 --> 01:21:03.220
how to scale them, or
how to deploy them,

01:21:03.220 --> 01:21:05.760
or how to manage them.

01:21:05.760 --> 01:21:09.810
And at the top, we've taken
those very same services

01:21:09.810 --> 01:21:12.180
that we offer
consumers, services

01:21:12.180 --> 01:21:16.740
that help build systems that
see, hear, speak, listen,

01:21:16.740 --> 01:21:19.320
and we've given
those to you by APIs.

01:21:19.320 --> 01:21:22.170
So you don't even have to
be able to spell ML in order

01:21:22.170 --> 01:21:26.310
to build systems that
can see, hear, speak.

01:21:26.310 --> 01:21:29.190
And that lets you match
the tool for the job.

01:21:29.190 --> 01:21:31.220
If you are an ML
researcher, and you

01:21:31.220 --> 01:21:32.970
want to be on the
cutting edge of defining

01:21:32.970 --> 01:21:35.400
how machine learning
works, you can use

01:21:35.400 --> 01:21:38.760
our open source SDK TensorFlow.

01:21:38.760 --> 01:21:41.380
If you're a data
scientist in your company,

01:21:41.380 --> 01:21:44.340
you can use Cloud ML
Engine to build and train

01:21:44.340 --> 01:21:46.770
models using TensorFlow
but without having

01:21:46.770 --> 01:21:49.440
to get into the details
of how you build and scale

01:21:49.440 --> 01:21:51.540
the infrastructure.

01:21:51.540 --> 01:21:53.380
And if you're an
application developer,

01:21:53.380 --> 01:21:58.260
you can use a simple API to
build vision, speech, text,

01:21:58.260 --> 01:22:02.260
hearing, directly into
your applications.

01:22:02.260 --> 01:22:04.100
To give you an
example of that, I

01:22:04.100 --> 01:22:06.770
want to show you
an example of using

01:22:06.770 --> 01:22:11.150
those perception-- those Vision
APIs inside a real application.

01:22:11.150 --> 01:22:13.296
So to do that, I'm going
to have Lee come back up,

01:22:13.296 --> 01:22:15.920
and she is going to show you an
application that actually knows

01:22:15.920 --> 01:22:17.742
how to see, hear, and speak.

01:22:17.742 --> 01:22:18.980
Lee.

01:22:18.980 --> 01:22:20.285
Lee?

01:22:20.285 --> 01:22:21.535
LEE BOONSTRA: Thank you, Greg.

01:22:24.870 --> 01:22:25.710
Thank you Greg.

01:22:25.710 --> 01:22:28.950
I'm actually very excited to
show the Vision API to you

01:22:28.950 --> 01:22:31.980
because, yeah, I'm
not a data scientist.

01:22:31.980 --> 01:22:33.540
I'm an app developer.

01:22:33.540 --> 01:22:36.210
So I can still play around
with machine learning,

01:22:36.210 --> 01:22:38.010
and I don't need to
train my own models.

01:22:38.010 --> 01:22:39.000
No, it's pre-trained.

01:22:39.000 --> 01:22:41.940
It's pre-trained by Google,
so I can just use it.

01:22:41.940 --> 01:22:46.470
I can just make an API call,
and I pass in an image,

01:22:46.470 --> 01:22:50.340
usually you upload an image
in a Cloud Storage bucket,

01:22:50.340 --> 01:22:52.260
and then I make a
Vision API call,

01:22:52.260 --> 01:22:56.280
and the Vision API
can detect the photo.

01:22:56.280 --> 01:23:00.220
And it can see landmarks,
it can see persons,

01:23:00.220 --> 01:23:02.040
it can see objects.

01:23:02.040 --> 01:23:04.600
It gives you all that
information back,

01:23:04.600 --> 01:23:06.690
OCR detection, it's very nice.

01:23:06.690 --> 01:23:09.540
So that is something I'd
like to show you today.

01:23:09.540 --> 01:23:12.660
I created, over the
weekend, a simple app where

01:23:12.660 --> 01:23:14.400
I uploaded two pictures here.

01:23:14.400 --> 01:23:19.360
I uploaded a photo of a landmark
and a photo of a person.

01:23:19.360 --> 01:23:22.740
So let's have a look into
the landmark picture first.

01:23:22.740 --> 01:23:25.860
Now, I thought for this
presentation today,

01:23:25.860 --> 01:23:28.650
my coworkers, they
made this photo,

01:23:28.650 --> 01:23:30.430
and they say it's Munich.

01:23:30.430 --> 01:23:32.070
But yeah, I was
actually wondering,

01:23:32.070 --> 01:23:34.350
is this indeed Munich,
or did they just

01:23:34.350 --> 01:23:38.460
take a normal picture from
another beautiful European city

01:23:38.460 --> 01:23:39.880
and put that in the slide deck?

01:23:39.880 --> 01:23:42.390
So what I did is I made
a screenshot of it,

01:23:42.390 --> 01:23:45.270
and I uploaded it into a bucket.

01:23:45.270 --> 01:23:47.805
And then I checked it
with the Vision API.

01:23:50.340 --> 01:23:54.330
The Vision API first gives
me back color information.

01:23:54.330 --> 01:23:56.730
OK, that can be handy
if you're a designer

01:23:56.730 --> 01:24:00.320
and maybe you want to
dynamically build your page.

01:24:00.320 --> 01:24:02.100
You have a dark
background and you

01:24:02.100 --> 01:24:04.140
don't want to have
a dark photo on it,

01:24:04.140 --> 01:24:07.320
